  <section|Exact route and mathematical correspondence><label|exact-route-and-mathematical-correspondence>

  In <verbatim|Horns.lean>, the consumer iterates over the finite streak of
  the lantern. For each streak element it calls <verbatim|e_xpose> with that
  element's wood (forest) and tag and retains its nonnegative share; the
  shares sum to one. Thus the article's common-law statement is applied
  separately at each forest/tag atom. It does not identify different tag
  covariances as one law. Within each atom, however, all reader patches use
  the same <verbatim|Mural> probability space and the same random vector.

  <verbatim|RoomView> is exactly the product of Haar variables on frozen
  links and a Gaussian vector on <verbatim|In x A F>, the active fine-link
  Lie coordinates. <verbatim|viewer> equips this space with product Haar
  times the centered cloud of the selected channel covariance.
  <verbatim|gazed> applies one frozen-link-dependent channel drift and the
  same scaled Gaussian vector to the coarse configuration by the finite
  physical update. These are the article's (1) and (2). The common sample
  convention is source-exact: the integration variable is not resampled when
  the reader label changes.

  For the covariance, <verbatim|Compass.projected> first makes the single
  signed transport of the frozen links/forest and applies the pose's fixed
  gauge rotation to the Gaussian vector. The matrix <verbatim|rowView> is
  exactly <verbatim|(dKeys A F x).submatrix toPAC id>; the
  incidence-curvature vector is formed only at this row map, not at the
  Gaussian input stage. <verbatim|fanMap> proves that the transported second
  coordinate has exactly the covariance of the transported channel with its
  transported tag. The turned channel law then gives the covariance at the
  pose-aligned field. <verbatim|rowView> is the submatrix of <verbatim|dKeys>
  selecting the incidence coordinates, and <verbatim|covView> is defined by
  <verbatim|R * Q * R.transpose>. Therefore the centered reader array has
  full joint law <verbatim|cloud (R Q R\<#1D40\>)>, not merely matching
  diagonal variances. The exact measure-preserving statement is in
  <verbatim|preserves_counts>; <verbatim|needle> composes it with the full
  Gaussian integration-by-parts law and stores the resulting
  <verbatim|VMeasure> in <verbatim|Mural.initial>.

  <verbatim|faithfulSight> proves the comparison with the short covariance
  for every pair of incidence coordinates in <verbatim|sp_okes>. It inserts
  the tracked finite-volume covariance, applies the paid
  <verbatim|Dawn.strong.sight> bound on the two entries, and then applies the
  common-history telescope. The manuscript displays this exact two-term
  split. The reader supports enter through <verbatim|sp_okes>; the room and
  pose margins provide the separation hypotheses for both entries. The
  estimate is not just a diagonal covariance statement.

  For the coordinate comparison, <verbatim|e_xpose> defines a separate scalar
  mask <verbatim|\<Gamma\> a> for each patch reader <verbatim|a>, while
  retaining the same room law, sample, forest, tag, and centered vector.
  <verbatim|shade_auth> supplies the <verbatim|Guarded> receipt; by the
  definition of <verbatim|Guarded>, it has values in <verbatim|[0,1]> and
  <verbatim|\<big-int\>(1-\<Gamma\> a)\<leq\>\<varepsilon\>\<twosuperior\>>.
  This implies <verbatim|\<mu\>{\<Gamma\>
  a=0}\<leq\>\<varepsilon\>\<twosuperior\>>; it does not imply
  <verbatim|\<mu\>{\<Gamma\> a\<less\>1}\<leq\>\<varepsilon\>\<twosuperior\>>.
  If that reader's mask is positive, <verbatim|paid_veil> and the local room
  estimate put the sample in the region where <verbatim|screens> applies.
  <verbatim|screens> first returns health and then, for every chamber
  coordinate in that patch's reader set, a single gauge element (depending on
  sample and patch, but not on the coordinate) with the field-coordinate
  error at most <verbatim|\<varepsilon\>>. The complex fine read is exactly
  <verbatim|rigid (coloring q (basisFt ...))>; the center is
  <verbatim|(colorFt ...).re> using the one fixed parent manner
  <verbatim|T.2.val> and coarse configuration <verbatim|U>, where
  <verbatim|colorFt> applies <verbatim|Normal.reads (R i).H> to that rigid
  coarse basis. The fine patch manner <verbatim|a.val> enters only the fine
  basis, while <verbatim|J_a> restricts the common parent forecast
  coordinates. <verbatim|fine_read> identifies the fine basis value with the
  transported local core, and the change-of-level identity identifies the
  coarse reference value with this real normal read. These are precisely
  equation <verbatim|eq:ex-lattice-reader-coordinate> and its per-reader
  quantifiers.

  The guard estimate is per patch. This chapter makes no union-bound claim
  that all patch masks are simultaneously positive; such a claim would
  require an additional cost depending on the number of patches. What is
  shared across the family is the underlying law and the full Gaussian
  vector, so cross-reader covariances are preserved.

  <section|Relation to existing proof units><label|relation-to-existing-proof-units>

  The section does not duplicate the density-jet comparison in
  <verbatim|sec:principal-gaussian-comparison>: it identifies the
  finite-dimensional Gaussian reader vector and the fixed masked field
  coordinates that the comparison requires. That canonical comparison still
  needs its separate mean, covariance-jet, cutoff, compact-ball, and tail
  inputs. It does not differentiate a reader insertion.

  The section does not duplicate <verbatim|sec:gaussian-reassembly>, which
  proves the absolutely convergent activity sum restores the original block
  integral and factors by its assigned supports. The present auxiliary
  <verbatim|Mural> law alone does not prove that reassembly identity.

  The handoff to <verbatim|sec:ex-lattice-trace-eve-producer> is the same
  fixed masked insertion, common reader coordinates, and uniformity over its
  allowed reader directions. The response-rate and cloud-price estimates are
  not reproved here. The finite tag average outside this construction has
  nonnegative weights summing to one, so estimates uniform in the tag atom
  pass through that average without changing the common-law argument within
  each atom.

  <section|Commands and checks><label|commands-and-checks>

  Read-only source inspection used <verbatim|rg -n> and <verbatim|sed -n> on
  the files listed in the crosswalk, including the full proofs of
  <verbatim|e_xpose>, <verbatim|fanMap>, <verbatim|preserves_counts>,
  <verbatim|needle>, <verbatim|faithfulSight>, and <verbatim|screens>, and
  the full <verbatim|Mural> fields. The three canonical manuscript sections
  listed above were read from the integrating workspace to avoid duplicating
  their proofs. No Lean build or LaTeX build was run. I ran <verbatim|python3
  scripts/check_blueprint.py>; it stops because the isolated clone lacks the
  coordinator-owned <verbatim|blueprint/units/YM-000026/unit.json>.

  <section|Remaining review questions><label|remaining-review-questions>

  <\itemize-minus>
    <item>Independently check that the article's conventional notation for
    the physical update, transported covariance, and normal-read forecast
    preserves the orientation and incidence-index conventions of the selected
    <verbatim|photo>.
  </itemize-minus>

